  {
    "heading": "3.1 Long-term Care Defined",
    "body_paragraphs": [
      {
        "type": "text",
        "content": "**Long-term care** is the help a person needs over an extended period to meet physical, mental, or emotional needs. You will see it range from light help with bathing or dressing to around-the-clock nursing. In practice, the key issue is not the setting alone, but whether the person needs ongoing personal care or supervision because of ADL limits or cognitive impairment. That matters when you explain benefits, because Medicare and Medicaid payment rules often depend on those triggers. For your clients, the same need may be met at home, in a nursing home, or in another care facility."
      },
      {
        "type": "important_callout",
        "label": "Important",
        "content": "Long-term care is a continuum, not a single service. When you discuss suitability, focus on how much help the client may need, where care could be delivered, and whether the need is likely to be temporary or ongoing."
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "heading": "3.1.1 Activities of Daily Living",
    "body_paragraphs": [
      {
        "type": "text",
        "content": "The six **activities of daily living (ADLs)** are the basic tasks people use to manage everyday life. They are eating, dressing, bathing, toileting, transferring, and continence. You use these terms often in long-term care conversations because a loss in two or more ADLs can help trigger benefits. In a client interview, that means asking practical questions, not medical jargon. For example, if a spouse says the insured can no longer bathe safely and needs help getting to the toilet, you are already hearing the kind of facts that matter for claim review."
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "heading": "3.1.2 Cognitive Impairment",
    "body_paragraphs": [
      {
        "type": "text",
        "content": "**Cognitive impairment** means a problem with orientation, memory, reasoning, or judgment. You may hear it described when a person gets lost in familiar places, forgets recent events, cannot handle simple reasoning, or fails to recognize danger. This matters because a severe cognitive problem can trigger long-term care benefits even when the person can still perform some ADLs. In practice, you should listen for safety issues and supervision needs. A family may say, for example, that a parent leaves the stove on and wanders outside at night. That is the kind of real-world detail that points to supervision, not just physical help."
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "heading": "3.2 Conditions Normally Requiring Long-term Care",
    "body_paragraphs": [
      {
        "type": "text",
        "content": "Long-term care can be needed for a short recovery period or for a permanent condition. You may see it after surgery, injury, or a long hospital stay, when the goal is to regain function. You also see it with chronic conditions such as dementia, Parkinson’s disease, stroke, multiple sclerosis, severe pain, or terminal illness. The practical point is that the need for care is driven by function, not just diagnosis. For example, a client recovering from a stroke may need therapy and daily help for months, while another client may need ongoing supervision because of dementia. Those are very different claims paths."
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "heading": "3.3 Types of Long-term Care",
    "body_paragraphs": [
      {
        "type": "text",
        "content": "Long-term care is usually grouped by the level of nursing help required. The main types are **skilled care**, **intermediate care**, and **custodial care**. Skilled and intermediate care focus on rehabilitation and returning function, while custodial care focuses on helping the person live as independently as possible. That distinction matters when you explain what a policy is designed to cover. A client who needs therapy after surgery may fit a different care level than someone who mainly needs help dressing, eating, or staying safe. The care type often drives both setting and cost."
      },
      {
        "type": "important_callout",
        "label": "Common Mistake",
        "content": "Do not treat every long-term care need as skilled care. Many claims involve custodial help, which is less medical but still very important to the client’s daily life."
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "heading": "3.3.1 Skilled Care",
    "body_paragraphs": [
      {
        "type": "text",
        "content": "**Skilled care** is care ordered by a physician and delivered by licensed professionals such as nurses or therapists. It is used when the condition is unstable, uncontrolled, or part of recovery after illness or surgery. The goal is rehabilitation, meaning the care is aimed at helping the person regain function. You should think of this as the higher-monitoring end of long-term care. For example, a client discharged after major surgery may need wound care, therapy, and close nursing oversight before going home. That is very different from simple help with meals or bathing."
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "heading": "3.3.2 Intermediate Care",
    "body_paragraphs": [
      {
        "type": "text",
        "content": "**Intermediate care** sits between skilled care and custodial care. It is still ordered by a physician and supervised by a registered nurse, but it is given less often than skilled care. The person is usually stable, yet still needs daily medical help and rehabilitation support. You may not hear this term as often today, but it still helps you understand the care continuum. In practice, imagine a patient who only needs dressing changes every few hours and light nursing oversight. That person may not need full skilled care, but the need is still more than basic personal assistance."
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "heading": "3.3.3 Custodial Care",
    "body_paragraphs": [
      {
        "type": "text",
        "content": "**Custodial care** is the most common long-term care need. It mainly helps with ADLs and supervision, especially when physical limits or cognitive impairment make independent living unsafe. Unlike skilled care, it is not rehabilitative. Its purpose is to help the person live as independently or safely as possible. In a real client conversation, this may sound like meal help, bathing assistance, reminders, or supervision to prevent wandering. That is why custodial care often becomes the core issue in long-term care planning, even when no major medical treatment is involved."
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "heading": "3.4 Long-term Care Settings",
    "body_paragraphs": [
      {
        "type": "text",
        "content": "Long-term care is delivered across a continuum, not in one place only. The main settings are nursing homes, alternate care facilities, community-based services, and care at home. The setting matters because it affects cost, level of supervision, and how much independence the person can keep. For example, a client who needs daily help but wants to stay home may use community services first, while a client with heavy supervision needs may move to a facility. In benefit discussions, you should also remember that Medicare and Medicaid payment rules can depend on the type of service and the setting."
      },
      {
        "type": "important_callout",
        "label": "Best Practice",
        "content": "When you explain long-term care, tie the care need to the setting. That helps clients understand why one person stays home with support while another needs a facility."
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "heading": "3.4.1 Nursing Home Defined",
    "body_paragraphs": [
      {
        "type": "text",
        "content": "A **nursing home** provides long-term care 24 hours a day under the supervision of licensed nursing staff. It may be a separate building or part of another facility, such as a wing of a hospital. Not every institution qualifies for long-term care benefits, so you should watch for exclusions like hospitals, clinics, or facilities mainly for mental illness or substance abuse treatment. Nursing homes may offer skilled care or custodial care, depending on their focus. In pricing discussions, they are usually the highest-cost option among the common care settings, which is why clients often compare them with assisted living before making a decision."
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "heading": "3.4.1.1 Services Provided in Nursing Homes",
    "body_paragraphs": [
      {
        "type": "text",
        "content": "A nursing home may provide either **skilled care** or **custodial care**, so the services depend on the facility’s purpose. That is why one nursing home may look very medical, while another feels more like supervised living. In cost discussions, nursing homes are usually more expensive than assisted living. A client comparing options may be surprised by that gap, especially when a private room is requested. Your job is to help the client understand that the level of nursing support, supervision, and room type all affect the bill. The service mix is just as important as the building."
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "heading": "3.4.1.2 Skilled Care",
    "body_paragraphs": [
      {
        "type": "text",
        "content": "In a nursing home, **skilled care** means the facility provides nursing or rehabilitation services to injured, sick, or disabled residents. The care must be delivered by licensed nursing personnel or under their supervision, and the facility must provide 24-hour nursing services with a registered nurse on duty daily. A physician must supervise medical care and be available for emergencies. This matters because it separates true medical-level care from simple residence-based support. For example, after a stroke or major surgery, a resident may need skilled nursing before returning home. That is the kind of setting where skilled care is used."
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "heading": "3.4.1.3 Custodial Care",
    "body_paragraphs": [
      {
        "type": "text",
        "content": "A custodial-care nursing home focuses on safety, daily support, and social needs rather than rehabilitation. Residents may receive meals, medication help, ADL assistance, and activities such as games or outings. This is useful when the person does not need intensive medical treatment but cannot live safely alone. In a claim or suitability discussion, that difference matters a lot. A family may expect a nursing home to mean constant medical care, but many residents mainly need supervision and help with routine living. That expectation gap is a common source of confusion."
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "heading": "3.4.1.4 Average U.S. Nursing Home Size",
    "body_paragraphs": [
      {
        "type": "text",
        "content": "Nursing homes serve a large but limited share of the long-term care market. You should not assume they provide most care just because they are the most visible setting. The source shows that nursing home residents are only part of the broader long-term care population, which also includes assisted living and home-based care. That matters when you help clients compare options, because many people will never enter a nursing home at all. The practical takeaway is simple: when clients ask about long-term care planning, you should think beyond the nursing home and look at the full care continuum."
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "heading": "3.4.2 Alternatives to Nursing Homes",
    "body_paragraphs": [
      {
        "type": "text",
        "content": "Alternative care facilities give clients another path when a nursing home is more care than they need. These settings are generally less costly and still provide ongoing support in one location. To qualify as an alternate care facility, the setting must provide 24-hour services for people with cognitive impairment or ADL limits, keep trained staff available at all times, serve three meals a day, maintain licensing or accreditation, arrange emergency medical support, and handle medications properly. Common examples include assisted living, Alzheimer’s facilities, and custodial care facilities. For many clients, this is the bridge between home and a nursing home."
      },
      {
        "type": "important_callout",
        "label": "Warning",
        "content": "Do not assume an alternate care facility provides the same medical intensity as a nursing home. The setting may be safer and more supportive, but it usually depends on outside medical providers for emergency or ongoing clinical care."
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "heading": "3.4.2.1 Assisted Living Facilities",
    "body_paragraphs": [
      {
        "type": "text",
        "content": "An **assisted living facility (ALF)** is often the middle ground between home care and a nursing home. Residents usually need help with meals, housekeeping, ADLs, or dementia-related support, but they do not need intensive skilled care. The care plan is updated as needs change, and basic services often include monitoring, housekeeping, medication management, recreation, security, and transportation. Extra services may cost more. In practice, that means an ALF can fit a client who still wants independence but needs daily support. It is a common choice when family caregiving is no longer enough, yet a nursing home still seems too restrictive."
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "heading": "3.4.2.2 Alzheimer’s Facilities Meet Alzheimer’s Patients’ Special Needs",
    "body_paragraphs": [
      {
        "type": "text",
        "content": "**Alzheimer’s facilities** and memory units are designed for people with Alzheimer’s disease or senile dementia who need a safer, more structured environment. These clients may be forgetful, disoriented, unable to reason well, or prone to wandering. That means the facility must reduce hazards, support routine, and help residents feel familiar and secure. In practice, a client with dementia may function better in a specialized unit than in a general care setting because the staff understands the condition and its behavior patterns. That is why the environment matters as much as the care plan."
      },
      {
        "type": "important_callout",
        "label": "Best Practice",
        "content": "When dementia is part of the case, look beyond basic supervision. Safety, orientation, and a calm routine are often just as important as meals and medication support."
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "heading": "3.4.2.3 Custodial Care Facilities",
    "body_paragraphs": [
      {
        "type": "text",
        "content": "Custodial care facilities provide a structured setting for people who need supervision and help with ADLs but not on-site medical services. They usually offer room, board, personal attendance, and a high level of oversight, while outside providers handle medical care. That makes them a bridge between home care and nursing home care. For your clients, this can be a practical choice when safety is the main concern but full nursing care is not needed. The mistake to avoid is assuming the word “facility” always means heavy medical treatment. Here, the emphasis is daily living support."
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "heading": "3.4.3 Community-Based Long-term Care Services",
    "body_paragraphs": [
      {
        "type": "text",
        "content": "Community-based long-term care helps people stay independent as long as possible. These services are delivered in the home or community and are meant to delay institutional care. They can include home nursing, therapy, homemaker help, meal support, adult day programs, respite care, case management, transportation, recreation, and home modifications. In practice, this is often the first step when a client still wants to remain at home but needs regular support. It also helps families manage work and caregiving. The main point is that long-term care is not always a facility decision; often it is a service plan decision."
      },
      {
        "type": "important_callout",
        "label": "Pro Tip",
        "content": "When clients want to stay at home, ask what support is already in place. The answer often shows whether home care, adult day care, or a facility is the better fit."
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "heading": "3.4.3.1 Paid Home Care Services",
    "body_paragraphs": [
      {
        "type": "text",
        "content": "Paid home care is care delivered in the client’s home by a compensated helper rather than a family member. It may be custodial, such as help with ADLs, companionship, transportation, or dementia supervision, or skilled, such as nursing or therapy after a stroke or heart attack. This matters because home care is often the least disruptive option for clients who want to remain where they live. In a real case, a recovering client may only need a nurse a few times a week and a home health aide for daily support. That mix is common, and it shows why home care is part of the long-term care continuum."
      }
    ]
  }
]

