        "content": "**Hospice care** is comfort-focused care for a terminally ill person with a life expectancy of about 6 to 12 months. It is usually delivered at home, but it can occur in many settings. A hospice team may include physicians, nurses, social workers, therapists, aides, pastors, and volunteers. The goal is not cure; it is to ease pain and distress for the client and family."
      },
      {
        "type": "important_callout",
        "label": "Important",
        "content": "Hospice is palliative care, so the emphasis is comfort and support. It is not the same thing as treatment aimed at recovery."
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "heading": "3.4.4 Informal Home Care",
    "body_paragraphs": [
      {
        "type": "text",
        "content": "**Informal home care** is unpaid help given at home by a relative, friend, or neighbor. No professional training is usually required, and the help often includes companionship, errands, medications, meals, transportation, bills, and contact with medical providers. In practice, this is the first layer of support many families use before they turn to paid long-term care."
      },
      {
        "type": "important_callout",
        "label": "Best Practice",
        "content": "When you discuss informal care, ask who is actually doing the work. That helps you spot the real caregiver and the possible need for future paid services."
