  {
    "heading": "3.5 Family Changes Affecting Long-term Care",
    "body_paragraphs": [
      {
        "type": "text",
        "content": "Long-term care demand is shaped by more than age. You also have to look at family change. Larger farm families once kept older relatives at home, but urban living, smaller households, greater mobility, and more women in the workforce changed that support system. As a result, families often have fewer nearby caregivers and turn to institutions or paid services sooner."
      },
      {
        "type": "text",
        "content": "For example, a client with two adult children may assume family will manage care later. If those children live in different states and work full time, the client may need formal care much sooner than expected. That shift matters when you explain why long-term care planning is not just about health; it is also about who is available to help."
      },
      {
        "type": "important_callout",
        "label": "Common Mistake",
        "content": "Do not assume family will always fill the gap. Smaller, more mobile families can mean less unpaid care when the need actually starts."
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "heading": "3.6 The Risk of Requiring Long-term Care",
    "body_paragraphs": [
      {
        "type": "text",
        "content": "You cannot predict exactly who will need long-term care, but several factors raise the risk. Age, longer life, being single, being a woman, unhealthy habits, chronic conditions, and family history all matter. A client with diabetes and arthritis may need help sooner than a healthy peer, especially if no informal caregiver is available."
