      {
        "type": "text",
        "content": "A good example is inflation protection. A client may resist it because it raises the premium, but without it the benefit can lose value over time. Another example is affordability after a life change. A client who can pay today may not be able to pay after a spouse dies or retirement income drops. You should also be careful when a client is close to the edge of affordability, because a lapse can waste years of premiums. The best practice is to match the policy to the client’s likely future, not just the current sales conversation."
